From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:14:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] document the auto-swap's window and the count's two clicks

HUMANLIKE.md's Timing section said the auto-swap's clicks were spaced by a redrawn
interval and stopped there; the rule about where they land is the other half of
the same thing and is now written next to it, with the half-applied state named as
the reason. The paragraph about the hand's counted edges said ButtonWritesTest's
list held "the elytra's firework" - it holds the file, and the file has two clicks
- so it says both, and names the window the auto-swap's clicks wait for.

Docs only; the change it describes is the commit before this one.
---
 HUMANLIKE.md | 19 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/HUMANLIKE.md b/HUMANLIKE.md
index 6e2a4f6f..29b610c5 100644
--- a/HUMANLIKE.md
+++ b/HUMANLIKE.md
@@ -70,6 +70,18 @@ ticks instead of stopping on the tick the movement stops asking (`Cadence.sprint
 a quarter faster); inventory moves, including the queued Elytra auto-swap clicks, are spaced by a redrawn
 interval rather than a fixed one.
 
+The auto-swap's three clicks are one *transaction* (`utils/InventoryTransaction`), and it is treated as
+one. A window click carries the id of the window it was written for and the game drops a click whose id is
+not the menu in front of the player, so a sequence fired with a chest or a crafting table open does not
+fail loudly - it stalls half applied, the spare elytra on the cursor and the worn one still on the back,
+which is worse than either end of the move. So the clicks wait for the player's own inventory to be the
+menu in front (`ElytraBehavior#tickInventoryTransactions` asks it); a sequence that has *not begun* when
+its window is gone is given up whole and silently, because nothing has happened, the items are where they
+were, and the job that wanted the swap asks again while the elytra is still worn; and a sequence that
+*has begun* waits the window out and finishes, however long that takes, because the half-applied state is
+the one worth avoiding. A person swapping an elytra with a chest open is holding the elytra out over the
+chest.
+
 ## Steering
 
 | what | where | the rule |
@@ -129,9 +141,10 @@ two mixin bridges that carry the aim and the elytra solver, each of which puts t
 POST. `CameraWritesTest` counts them, so a fifth file fails the build with this list beside it. The other two
 edges of the hand are counted the same way: `HandWritesTest` counts the files that may write the selected
 slot, and `ButtonWritesTest` counts the files that may press a mouse button or swing an arm - the two
-helpers, the inventory swap, the idle fidget and the elytra's firework - asserting beside each one the beat
-it is on the list for and pinning the wait itself: the gate `InputOverrideHandler` computes from the
-inventory's wind-up and the question each helper asks before it presses. A click with no reaction in front of
+helpers, the inventory swap, the idle fidget, and the elytra's two, the firework and the window clicks of
+the auto-swap - asserting beside each one the beat it is on the list for and pinning the wait itself: the
+gate `InputOverrideHandler` computes from the inventory's wind-up, the question each helper asks before it
+presses, and the window the auto-swap's clicks wait for (`InventoryTransaction`). A click with no reaction in front of
 it, or one pressed with the hand between items, cannot be added without saying so.
 
